b.add(685,'ode-state','Identify the numerical state and its derivative','In joint_ode, theta_dot=state[0] and the returned list is [theta_ddot]; what does the solver integrate?', ['One angular-speed state whose derivative is angular acceleration','A two-component position-and-speed state','One torque state whose derivative is speed','The armature current and motor position simultaneously'],0,'The printed function has one state variable, angular velocity; position is not included as a separate integrated state.',['This matches the state read and returned derivative.','The code would need two state entries and derivatives for that representation.','The state is explicitly assigned to theta_dot, not torque.','No electrical current state or position state is present.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(685,'ode-state','Distinguish state order from position-model order','How can the printed one-state speed ODE be consistent with a second-order position equation?', ['Angular speed is the first derivative of position, so including position would add the kinematic equation θdot=ω','A one-state solver automatically stores a second hidden physical state for every problem','The inertia term disappears whenever solve_ivp is used','The speed state is actually the second derivative of current'],0,'The mechanical position equation is second order, while its autonomous speed subsystem is first order; integrating position as well requires another state.',['This explains the two descriptions without changing the physics.','A hidden physical position state is not supplied automatically.','The printed ODE explicitly retains effective inertia.','The code and physical units identify angular speed.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(685,'solver-arguments','Read the no-load parameter tuple','Why does the no-load solve_ivp call pass args=(I_m,b_m,0.0,0.0,1.0,tau_m)?', ['It sets load inertia and load damping to zero and sets the gear ratio to one','It sets motor inertia and motor damping to zero and leaves load parameters unchanged','It sets motor torque to zero and simulates free decay only','It sets the initial position to zero and the initial speed to one'],0,'The tuple is ordered I_m,b_m,I,b,eta,tau_m, so the two zeros remove external load parameters and eta=1 makes motor and load coordinates coincide.',['This follows the function signature.','The motor parameters remain the first two nonzero arguments.','tau_m remains the final torque argument.','Initial state is passed separately, not inside args.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(685,'solver-output','Interpret sol.y[0] and numerical differentiation','For this one-state solve, what do sol.y[0] and np.gradient(sol.y[0],sol.t) represent?', ['Sampled angular speed and an approximate angular acceleration using the sample times','Sampled angle and exact torque at all times','The first scalar speed only and the full exact symbolic derivative','All solver parameters and their gradients with respect to gear ratio'],0,'The first row of sol.y holds the single speed state across output times, and np.gradient estimates its time derivative numerically.',['This states both array meanings and approximation.','No angle state or torque differentiation is requested.','Row indexing returns a time series, and numerical gradients are not exact symbolic derivatives.','The arrays are solution samples rather than parameter lists.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add([685,686],'no-load-steady','Find the no-load equilibrium speed','With I_m=0.01 kg·m², b_m=0.1 N·m·s/rad and constant τ_m=2 N·m, what no-load steady angular speed follows?', ['20 rad/s','200 rad/s','0.2 rad/s','0 rad/s'],0,'At steady speed acceleration is zero, so viscous damping balances torque and ω_ss=τ_m/b_m=20 rad/s.',['This uses the steady damping balance.','This divides torque by inertia, which gives acceleration rather than steady speed.','This multiplies torque by damping instead of dividing.','A nonzero applied torque with finite damping has a nonzero equilibrium speed.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='easy',steps=['Set I_m dω/dt=0 at steady speed.','Solve b_mω=τ_m to get 2/0.1=20 rad/s.'],formulas=[formula('ω_ss=τ_m/b_m',{'ω_ss':'no-load steady angular speed','τ_m':'constant applied motor torque','b_m':'motor viscous damping'},{'ω_ss':'rad/s','τ_m':'N·m','b_m':'N·m·s/rad'})])
b.add([685,686],'no-load-transient','Derive the no-load speed rise from rest','For the same no-load motor starting from rest, which speed function solves 0.01ωdot+0.1ω=2?', ['ω(t)=20(1−exp(−10t)) rad/s','ω(t)=20exp(−10t) rad/s','ω(t)=200t rad/s for all t despite damping','ω(t)=20(1−exp(−0.1t)) rad/s'],0,'The time constant is I_m/b_m=0.1 s and the steady speed is 20 rad/s; the zero initial condition selects the rising exponential.',['This satisfies both the differential equation and initial condition.','This starts at 20 and decays, contradicting the stated initial rest and torque input.','This ignores the increasing damping torque.','This inverts the time constant in the exponent.'],typ='derivation',steps=['Compute steady speed 2/0.1=20 rad/s.','Compute time constant 0.01/0.1=0.1 s.','Apply ω(0)=0 to the first-order solution.'],formulas=[formula('ω(t)=ω_ss+(ω_0−ω_ss)exp(−t/T_m); T_m=I_m/b_m',{'ω':'motor speed','ω_ss':'steady speed','ω_0':'initial speed','t':'time','T_m':'mechanical speed time constant','I_m':'motor inertia','b_m':'motor damping'},{'ω,ω_ss,ω_0':'rad/s','t,T_m':'s','I_m':'kg·m²','b_m':'N·m·s/rad'})])
b.add([685,686],'acceleration-estimate','Explain an initial numerical-gradient discrepancy','For ω(t)=20(1−exp(−10t)) sampled at 0 and 0.01 s, the default first-endpoint difference is about 190.33 rad/s²; how does this compare with the analytic initial acceleration?', ['The analytic value is 200 rad/s², while the finite-step endpoint estimate is slightly lower','The analytic value must equal 190.33 exactly because np.gradient is symbolic differentiation','The analytic value is 20 rad/s² because steady speed and initial acceleration are identical','The finite difference proves the inertia term was omitted from the ODE'],0,'The exact derivative at zero is 200; the first finite difference averages a derivative that is already decreasing over the 0.01 s interval.',['This explains the numerical approximation without inventing a physics error.','np.gradient estimates derivatives from finite samples.','Speed and acceleration have different units and values.','The observed difference is expected for finite sampling, not evidence that inertia was omitted.'],typ='numerical',assumptions=['The 0.01 s step is explicitly supplied for this question; the slide does not print the t_eval construction.','Use the default first-order endpoint gradient on the analytic sample values.'],steps=['Differentiate ω to obtain 200exp(−10t), giving 200 at t=0.','Compute [20(1−exp(−0.1))−0]/0.01≈190.325.'])
b.add([685,686],'no-load-plot','Read the speed and acceleration plots together','Which interpretation matches the no-load speed-rise and acceleration-decay plots?', ['The motor approaches a nonzero constant speed while angular acceleration tends to zero','The motor approaches zero speed while angular acceleration remains nonzero','The position reaches a fixed commanded angle because acceleration decays','The torque input must tend to zero because acceleration tends to zero'],0,'Viscous damping eventually balances the constant input torque, leaving nonzero steady speed and zero acceleration.',['This matches the curves and torque balance.','This reverses the displayed speed behavior.','A constant nonzero speed means position continues to change; no position target is controlled here.','The torque remains nonzero to balance damping.'],typ='diagram',difficulty='easy')
b.add(685,'code-completeness','Identify the missing standalone program context','What must be supplied before the printed no-load excerpt can run as a standalone Python program?', ['Imports for NumPy and solve_ivp, plus definitions of t_span, theta_dot_0 and t_eval','A replacement for the already printed joint_ode definition only','A new value for I_m because no motor inertia is shown','A new value for tau_m because the source never specifies motor torque'],0,'The excerpt shows the model and parameter values but not the imports or the time-grid and initial-state definitions; plotting calls are also absent from the shown excerpt.',['These are actual unresolved names in the printed code.','The ODE function is already shown.','I_m=0.01 is printed.','tau_m=2.0 is printed.'],typ='source-reconciliation',conflict='The shown Python excerpt omits imports, the integration interval, the initial-state definition, the output-time vector and plotting commands; a reconstructed executable program must label those additions.')
b.add(687,'gear-code-trace','Trace the gear-relation program output','The gear code sets eta=10, reuses a 20 rad/s motor steady speed and sets tau_motor=2 N·m; which values does that code compute?', ['theta_dot_load=2 rad/s and tau_load=20 N·m','theta_dot_load=200 rad/s and tau_load=0.2 N·m','theta_dot_load=20 rad/s and tau_load=2 N·m','theta_dot_load=2 rad/s and tau_load=2 N·m'],0,'The printed operations divide the stored speed by eta and multiply the stored torque by eta.',['This is the literal code arithmetic, not an independent loaded-operating-point validation.','This reverses both printed operations.','This ignores the ratio in both operations.','This applies the speed operation but omits torque multiplication.'],typ='code_output',difficulty='easy',assumptions=['The question asks what the printed program computes, not whether both numbers describe a physically available loaded operating point with motor losses.'])
b.add([685,687],'gear-operating-point','Do not mistake rescaled no-load data for a loaded simulation','Why does the gear-relation example not establish that 20 N·m can be delivered to an external load while the motor still runs at its prior no-load 20 rad/s?', ['It reuses a no-load solution, and at that speed the 2 N·m electromagnetic torque already balances the modeled motor friction','The arithmetic 20/10=2 is false','An ideal gear can never multiply a transmitted torque','The gear ratio alone forces every DC motor to stop'],0,'The code does not re-solve a loaded torque balance; using the already-consumed electromagnetic torque as an additional transmitted load torque would double-count the available power.',['This distinguishes port torque from the torque already used by motor losses.','The speed division is arithmetically correct.','Ideal gears can trade speed for transmitted torque while conserving power.','The result depends on the full torque balance, not a universal stop rule.'],typ='source-reconciliation',conflict='The gear example rescales the previous no-load speed and the full applied motor torque, without separately accounting for the motor friction that already consumes that torque at no load.',steps=['At 20 rad/s, b_mω=0.1×20=2 N·m.','This already balances the applied 2 N·m in the no-load model.','A loaded operating point requires a new torque balance or a clearly separated ideal-port illustration.'])
b.add(688,'loaded-solver-loop','Distinguish the loaded loop from the earlier rescaling','What changes in the gear-ratio sweep compared with the simple gear-relation example?', ['The sweep calls solve_ivp again for each ratio with nonzero load inertia and damping, then converts load speed to motor speed','The sweep merely changes plot colors while using the exact same unmodified speed vector for every ratio','The sweep removes motor inertia and damping from joint_ode','The sweep solves only for gear angle and does not integrate speed'],0,'Each eta_val is passed into the mechanical ODE with I=1 and b=0.05, so the modeled transient changes with the ratio.',['This follows the loop and parameter tuple.','The code invokes a fresh solve for every listed ratio.','The effective terms still include I_m and b_m.','The state remains angular speed.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(688,'loaded-solver-loop','Trace the ratio/color pairing','In zip([1,5,20,50],["steelblue","darkorange","forestgreen","tomato"]), which ratio belongs to the forestgreen curve?', ['20','5','50','1'],0,'zip pairs entries at the same list position; forestgreen is paired with the third ratio, 20.',['This follows the aligned third entries.','This is paired with darkorange.','This is paired with tomato.','This is paired with steelblue.'],typ='code_output',difficulty='easy')
b.add(688,'loaded-effective-values','Calculate one gear-sweep model','For I=1, I_m=0.01, b=0.05, b_m=0.1 and η=5, which effective inertia and damping are used?', ['1.25 kg·m² and 2.55 N·m·s/rad','1.05 kg·m² and 0.55 N·m·s/rad','25.01 kg·m² and 1.35 N·m·s/rad','1.25 kg·m² and 0.15 N·m·s/rad'],0,'Both motor terms are multiplied by 25, giving 1+0.25=1.25 and 0.05+2.5=2.55.',['This reflects both motor parameters to the load coordinate.','This uses η rather than η².','This reflects the load terms in the wrong direction.','This reflects inertia but leaves motor damping unscaled.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='easy',steps=['Compute η²=25.','Compute J=1+25×0.01=1.25.','Compute B=0.05+25×0.1=2.55.'],formulas=[eq])
b.add(688,'loaded-steady-speed','Derive the motor steady speed in the sweep','For the load-coordinate ODE Jω_load_dot+Bω_load=ητ_m, which motor steady-speed formula is used?', ['ω_motor,ss=η²τ_m/(b+η²b_m)','ω_motor,ss=τ_m/(I+η²I_m)','ω_motor,ss=ητ_m/(b+η²b_m)','ω_motor,ss=(b+η²b_m)/(η²τ_m)'],0,'At steady state load speed is ητ_m/B; multiplying by η converts it to motor speed.',['This includes the second ratio from the coordinate conversion.','Inertia affects the transient, not the viscous steady-speed balance.','This is the load steady speed before conversion.','This is the reciprocal and has incorrect speed dimensions.'],typ='derivation',steps=['Set load acceleration to zero.','Solve ω_load,ss=ητ_m/B.','Multiply by η to obtain motor speed.'],formulas=[formula('ω_load,ss=ητ_m/B; ω_motor,ss=η²τ_m/B',{'ω_load,ss':'steady load angular speed','ω_motor,ss':'steady motor angular speed','η':'motor/load speed ratio','τ_m':'motor input torque','B':'effective load-coordinate damping'},{'speeds':'rad/s','η':'dimensionless','τ_m':'N·m','B':'N·m·s/rad'})])
b.add(688,'loaded-steady-speed','Calculate the eta-five motor asymptote','For the preceding η=5 model with τ_m=2 N·m and B=2.55 N·m·s/rad, what is the motor steady speed?', ['Approximately 19.61 rad/s','Approximately 3.92 rad/s','Approximately 40 rad/s','Approximately 0.051 rad/s'],0,'The load steady speed is 10/2.55≈3.92157 rad/s, so motor speed is five times that, 19.60784 rad/s.',['This includes the load-to-motor conversion.','This is the load speed, not motor speed.','This divides torque by load damping alone.','This is approximately the reciprocal of the correct motor speed.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Calculate load speed 5×2/2.55≈3.92157.','Multiply by 5 to obtain approximately 19.60784 rad/s.'])
b.add(688,'plot-asymptote','Distinguish an asymptote label from the plotted finite-time value','The η=1 legend lists a 13.3 rad/s steady motor speed, but its curve is about 7 rad/s at 5 s; which interpretation is correct?', ['The legend gives the long-time asymptote, while the plotted response has not reached it by 5 s','The graph necessarily contradicts the ODE because the last sample must equal steady state','The legend is a load angle in radians rather than a speed','The curve is forced to stop at 7 rad/s permanently after 5 s'],0,'For η=1, J/B=1.01/0.15≈6.73 s, so five seconds is less than one time constant and the transient is still rising.',['This explains the difference using the model time constant.','A finite integration window need not reach the asymptote.','The legend explicitly labels motor steady angular speed.','The plot ending does not impose a physical stop.'],typ='diagram',steps=['Compute T=J/B=1.01/0.15≈6.733 s.','Five seconds is less than T.','The analytic rise gives (2/0.15)(1−exp(−5/T))≈6.99 rad/s.'])
b.add(688,'gear-limit','Evaluate the large-ratio steady-speed limit','With fixed positive b_m and τ_m in the loaded model, what happens as η tends to infinity?', ['Motor steady speed tends to τ_m/b_m, while load steady speed tends to zero','Both motor and load steady speeds grow without bound','Motor steady speed tends to zero, while load speed tends to τ_m/b_m','Both speeds tend to the same nonzero value regardless of η'],0,'Divide the motor expression by η² to obtain τ_m/(b/η²+b_m); load speed is this motor speed divided by η.',['These limits follow from the damping model.','Reflected motor damping prevents unlimited speed growth.','This reverses the coordinate relation.','The ratio between speeds remains η.'],typ='derivation',assumptions=['Keep τ_m>0 and b_m>0 fixed; use the stated rigid viscous model, not a claim about real motor voltage limits.'],steps=['Write ω_motor,ss=τ_m/(b/η²+b_m).','Take η→∞ to obtain τ_m/b_m.','Divide by η for the load-speed limit.'])
b.add(688,'initial-acceleration-optimum','Find the ratio maximizing initial load acceleration','From rest, with I=1 kg·m², I_m=0.01 kg·m² and τ_m=2 N·m, which positive η maximizes a_load(0)=ητ_m/(I+η²I_m) if η is treated as continuous?', ['10','1','20','The acceleration grows without bound as η increases'],0,'Differentiating gives a factor I−η²I_m in the numerator, so the positive maximum occurs at η=sqrt(I/I_m)=10.',['This makes the derivative zero and separates increasing from decreasing acceleration.','This does not balance the two inertia contributions.','This is beyond the maximum; the reflected motor inertia is already dominant.','The expression tends to zero as η grows without bound.'],typ='derivation',difficulty='hard',assumptions=['Treat η as a positive continuous design variable and ignore practical motor, gear and voltage limits; initial speed is zero, so viscous damping contributes no initial torque.'],steps=['Differentiate ητ_m/(I+η²I_m).','Set the numerator τ_m(I−η²I_m) to zero.','Solve η=sqrt(1/0.01)=10 and check the derivative changes from positive to negative.'],formulas=[formula('a_0(η)=ητ_m/(I+η²I_m); η_opt=√(I/I_m)',{'a_0':'initial load angular acceleration','η':'motor/load speed ratio','τ_m':'constant motor torque','I,I_m':'load and motor inertia','η_opt':'ratio maximizing the stated initial acceleration'},{'a_0':'rad/s²','η,η_opt':'dimensionless','τ_m':'N·m','I,I_m':'kg·m²'})])
b.add([689,690],'vectorized-parameters','Trace the effective-parameter array row','The final example uses I=2, I_m=0.01, b=0.2 and b_m=0.05; which pair appears for η=30?', ['I_eff=11 kg·m² and b_eff=45.2 N·m·s/rad','I_eff=2.3 kg·m² and b_eff=1.7 N·m·s/rad','I_eff=11 kg·m² and b_eff=90.2 N·m·s/rad','I_eff=9 kg·m² and b_eff=45 N·m·s/rad'],0,'The squared ratio is 900, giving 2+900×0.01=11 and 0.2+900×0.05=45.2.',['This uses the final example’s parameters and includes both load offsets.','This uses the unsquared ratio.','This reuses the earlier b_m=0.1 rather than the updated 0.05.','This omits the load inertia and load damping.'],typ='code_output',steps=['Square 30 to obtain 900.','Compute both vectorized expressions with the updated final-example constants.'])
b.add(689,'vectorized-parameters','Identify the unused parameter in the displayed array expressions','Which displayed parameter does not affect I_eff_arr or b_eff_arr in the shown calculation?', ['tau_m=3.0','I_m=0.01','b=0.2','eta_values'],0,'The array expressions use inertia, damping and squared gear ratios; torque is assigned but is not used in either expression.',['This variable is absent from both displayed formulas.','Motor inertia changes I_eff_arr.','Load damping changes b_eff_arr.','The ratio array changes both outputs.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(689,'vectorized-parameters','Explain the elementwise array computation','For eta_values=np.array([1,2,5,10,20,30,50]), what does I+(eta_values**2)*I_m return?', ['A seven-element array, with each ratio squared and scaled before adding I','One scalar formed by summing all ratios before squaring','The original ratio array repeated I_m times','A two-dimensional matrix product of all ratio pairs'],0,'NumPy exponentiation and scalar arithmetic operate elementwise on this one-dimensional array.',['This matches the vectorized expression.','No sum operation is present.','Multiplication by a scalar scales numeric entries rather than repeating an ndarray.','The expression does not request an outer or matrix product.'],typ='code',difficulty='easy')
b.add(690,'quadratic-offset','Distinguish a quadratic contribution from total-parameter ratios','In the final table, η rises from 10 to 20; what happens to I_eff=2+0.01η²?', ['The reflected motor contribution quadruples from 1 to 4, while total inertia doubles from 3 to 6','Both the reflected contribution and total inertia quadruple','Both the reflected contribution and total inertia only double','The total inertia remains 2 because only the gear changes'],0,'The η²-dependent part scales by four, but the unchanged load offset means the total need not quadruple.',['This correctly separates the offset and variable term.','The nonzero load offset prevents a fourfold total change in this case.','The reflected term is quadratic rather than linear.','The effective inertia includes the gear-reflected motor term.'],typ='scenario',steps=['At η=10, the reflected term is 1 and the total is 3.','At η=20, the reflected term is 4 and the total is 6.'])
b.add([689,690],'dominance-threshold','Locate the first listed inertia-dominant ratio','For I=2 and I_m=0.01, which is the first ratio in [1,2,5,10,20,30,50] where η²I_m exceeds I?', ['20','10','2','50'],0,'The inequality η²×0.01>2 requires η>sqrt(200)≈14.14; the first listed ratio beyond it is 20.',['This is the first listed value above the threshold.','At 10, reflected motor inertia is 1, still below 2.','At 2, reflected motor inertia is only 0.04.','Fifty exceeds the threshold but is not the first listed value.'],typ='numerical',steps=['Solve η²>200.','Take the positive root η>14.142.','Select the first listed ratio above that value.'])
b.add([689,690],'damping-threshold','Compare the separate damping and inertia thresholds','In the final example, at what ratio does reflected motor damping equal the load damping b=0.2 when b_m=0.05?', ['η=2','η=4','η=sqrt(200)','η=20'],0,'Equality requires η²×0.05=0.2, so η²=4 and the positive gear ratio is 2; this is different from the inertia-dominance threshold.',['This satisfies the stated damping equality.','This forgets to take the square root.','This is the inertia equality threshold, not damping.','This is a listed inertia-dominant ratio, not the damping equality.'],typ='numerical',difficulty='easy',steps=['Solve η²=b/b_m=0.2/0.05=4.','Take the positive root to obtain η=2.'])
